the roman catholic church and the papacy  represents a deep wound for many  residential school survivors  and their families i've had to go to  therapy ever since i was a little kid  just because of the you know the  domestic violence and sexual violence uh  spiritual and emotional violence that  you know i grew up with in my family um  and uh you know and and and that's a  shared experience clayton thomas mueller  is very open about the intergenerational  effects of residential schools his  mother and father are both survivors for  him processing the pain has happened  through storytelling a pillar of his  cree culture a culture he had to learn  to love i remember telling kids in  kindergarten in grade one that i was  italian  because i felt so afraid to let people  know i was native an estimated 150  thousand children were exposed to the  abusive culturally and spiritually  destructive system that operated for  more than a century in canada  most of the churches were run by  catholics the government of canada  sincerely apologizes and asks the  forgiveness that apology from former  prime minister stephen harper came in  2008 a year later indigenous delegates  traveled to vatican city to meet with  pope benedict xvi he expressed sorrow  and offered his sympathies but did not  issue a formal apology  wob canoe was there with his father a  survivor my dad extended an eagle  feather to him knowing that that's a  symbol of indigenous spirituality and  the pope accepted it and a kind of uh a  a gesture i guess of mutual respect and  goodwill  and that interaction i think really you  know  helped my dad to find  some measure of closure canoe's uncle  also a survivor will be part of a group  of indigenous canadians in vatican city  for a historic visit beginning monday  the first to meet with the pope will be  the metis delegation  the hope this time is that the trip will  end with an official papal apology  crystal gomanson global news london
 

